TI  - 'Not a Replacement, but a Companion': The Paradigm Shift in AI-Assisted Learning Among Engineering Students in Bangladesh
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 2026 14th International Conference on Information and Education Technology (ICIET)
SP  - 577
EP  - 583
DO  - 10.1109/iciet69664.2026.11561611
AB  - AI-based chat tools have become integral learning companions for engineering students, supporting coding, problem-solving, research, and technical writing. This study adopts a mixed-method, student-centered approach to examine usage patterns, preferences, perceived reliability, and academic impact of AI chat tools among engineering students. Data were collected from 150 students across 24 universities in Bangladesh through structured questionnaires and semi-structured interviews, followed by quantitative analysis and qualitative thematic coding. The findings show that these tools are deeply embedded in students' academic routines, primarily driven by time efficiency and reduced manual effort. Usage evolves with academic maturity: early-year students tend toward replacement-based reliance, while senior and graduate students use these tools more strategically as companions to support and enhance their learning rather than replace it. While students report improved efficiency and problem-solving support, concerns remain regarding hallucinations, bias, reduced independent thinking, and academic integrity. Overall, the results highlight the need for stronger AI literacy, clearer institutional guidelines, and domainspecific academic AI systems to ensure a balanced integration that supports both efficiency and critical thinking. © 2026 IEEE.
