TI  - VR-Enhanced Situational Teaching with Digital Competitive Gaming: Examining Comprehensive Physical Fitness and Psychological Adaptations
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of 2024 International Conference on Sports Technology and Performance Analysis, ICSTPA 2024
SP  - 494
EP  - 501
DO  - 10.1145/3723936.3724014
AB  - Background: Contemporary educational paradigms have undergone substantial transformation due to digitalization, presenting significant challenges for physical education pedagogy. While Virtual Reality (VR) technology demonstrates considerable potential for creating immersive educational experiences, the empirical validation of its integration with competitive digital gaming elements in tertiary physical education remains insufficiently explored. Objective: This investigation sought to evaluate the efficacy of VR-enhanced situational teaching methodology incorporating competitive digital gaming elements on both comprehensive physical fitness parameters and psychological adaptations among university students. Methods: The study employed a randomized controlled design comprising fifty undergraduate students (19 males, 31 females; Mage = 20.0 years, SD = 1.54) from a comprehensive university. Participants were randomly allocated to either an experimental cohort (n = 25) experiencing VR-enhanced instruction through Meta Quest 2 systems with modified PUBG environments, or a control cohort (n = 25) receiving traditional physical education. The eight-week intervention protocol incorporated standardized assessments of physical fitness parameters, Exercise Self-Efficacy Scale measurements, and Group Environment Questionnaire evaluations at baseline (T0), mid-intervention (T1), and post-intervention (T2). Results: Statistical analyses revealed significant enhancement in vital capacity within the experimental cohort (F(1,48) = 4.27, p < 0.05, ηp2 = 0.13), accompanied by substantial improvements in exercise self-efficacy (F(1,48) = 7.82, p < 0.01, ηp2 = 0.140). Group environment measures demonstrated significant main effects for both group (F(1,48) = 6.94, p < 0.01, ηp2 = 0.126) and time (F(2,96) = 8.67, p < 0.01, ηp2 = 0.153), with the experimental cohort exhibiting progressive enhancement from baseline (M = 53.45 ± 8.23) through final assessment (M = 58.92 ± 7.89). Structural equation modeling analyses identified exercise self-efficacy as the predominant predictor of physical fitness outcomes (β = 0.385, p < 0.01), while group environment demonstrated a significant but more modest relationship (β = 0.264, p < 0.05). Conclusions: VR simulative situational teaching method is effective in improving university students’ vital capacity, exercise self-efficacy, and group environment. In other words, VR as an intervention works mainly on psychological factors and in that sense, the technology is a practical tool to modernize physical education while keeping it true to its goal. These results offer significant empirical basis for rational integrating of digital gaming elements into tertiary education of physical culture. © 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
