TI  - Experimental design for low-frequency transmission line protection for offshore flexible fractional-frequency transmission system
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
JO  - Experimental Technology and Management
T2  - Experimental Technology and Management
VL  - 43
IS  - 1
SP  - 177
EP  - 187
DO  - 10.16791/j.cnki.sjg.2026.01.022
AB  - [Objective] The strategic expansion of offshore wind power generation constitutes a critical pathway for achieving China’s dual-carbon objectives, with flexible fractional-frequency transmission system (FFFTS) emerging as a technologically advanced solution for large-scale long-distance power transmission. These systems effectively integrate the advantages of both AC and DC transmission technologies, thereby addressing the key challenges in offshore energy evacuation. However, the reliable operation of FFFTSs fundamentally depends on the performance of protection schemes for their low-frequency transmission lines. A significant challenge arises from the system’s inherent weak-infeed characteristics, stemming from the low inertia of wind power sources and the complexity of converter control strategies. These factors consequently cause substantial degradation in the sensitivity of conventional current differential protection schemes. This decline in sensitivity poses serious risks to system security, potentially resulting in protection failures. Therefore, this study aims to develop a novel protection scheme that addresses these limitations by ensuring fast, accurate, and reliable fault identification in FFFTS environments. [Methods] A detailed FFFTS model was developed using PSCAD/EMTDC. The model incorporated the dynamic characteristics of key components, including the Modular Multilevel Matrix Converter. Building upon this foundation, this study employed composite sequence network theory to conduct a rigorous analysis of fault characteristics, specifically examining the limitations of traditional current differential protection under weak-infeed conditions. The analysis quantitatively demonstrated how system parameters affect protection sensitivity, particularly highlighting the challenges associated with high-impedance faults. In response to these findings, a novel protection scheme based on the Markov transition field (MTF) singular spectrum distribution was proposed. The methodology converts one-dimensional current samples from both line terminals into two-dimensional MTF matrices via quantile-based discretization, effectively transforming temporal current variations into spatial patterns that preserve critical dynamic characteristics. Singular value decomposition was then applied to extract energy distribution features from these matrices, followed by the Hausdorff distance metric to quantify discrepancies between the singular spectra obtained from both line terminals, thereby establishing a robust protection criterion. [Results] Comprehensive simulation studies validated the effectiveness of the proposed protection scheme under diverse operating conditions. The results demonstrated that the MTF-based method successfully distinguished various internal faults (e.g., AG, AB, ABG, and ABC). The scheme maintained excellent stability for external faults, effectively preventing maloperation. The protection scheme exhibited remarkable robustness, maintaining correct operation under challenging conditions, including high transition resistances up to 200 Ω, with Hausdorff distances remaining well above the operation threshold. Furthermore, the scheme demonstrated strong immunity to current transformer sampling errors, performing reliably even under extreme measurement inaccuracies of ±20%. Collectively, these results demonstrate superior performance in weak-infeed scenarios compared with conventional protection methods. [Conclusions] The proposed MTF-based protection scheme effectively resolves the critical sensitivity challenges associated with traditional current differential protection in FFFTS weak-infeed environments. This method achieves enhanced discrimination between internal and external faults while maintaining robustness against various system disturbances. This research contributes a technically advanced solution for securing offshore wind power integration, particularly addressing the protection challenges posed by weak-infeed characteristics and complex control strategies. Beyond its technical contributions, the system modeling and experimental design offer significant educational value, serving as a practical platform for cultivating professional skills in power system protection within electrical engineering disciplines. © 2026 Tsinghua University Press. All rights reserved.
