TI  - FRAME: A Failure-Aware Resilient Approach for Mastery in Pre-Engineering Education
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - SoutheastCon 2026
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/southeastcon63549.2026.11476396
AB  - Persistent attrition in engineering programs indicates that many students enter postsecondary education without adequate preparation for the cognitive demands of engineering problem solving [1],[2]. Although K-12 STEM initiatives have increased exposure and engagement, fewer intentionally cultivate engineering-specific competencies such as iterative design, performance accountability, and failure-informed learning [3],[11]. This paper introduces FRAME (Failure-Aware Resilient Approach for Mastery in Engineering Pathways), a structured pre-engineering instructional framework that embeds these competencies within K-12 STEM environments through constraint-driven design challenges, structured remediation, and reassessment. Failure is positioned as an instructional asset within an iterative learning cycle supported by consistent performancebased evaluation, enabling conceptual mastery without grade inflation. Unlike exploratory STEM models that emphasize activity completion, FRAME prioritizes iterative refinement, accountability, and decision-making under constraints. Presented as a pilot-ready model, the framework outlines instructional architecture, educator support mechanisms, and assessment strategies aligned with engineering education principles. While large-scale outcome data are not reported, the paper defines measurable performance indicators suitable for future empirical validation and offers a transferable pathway for strengthening continuity between early STEM learning and postsecondary engineering expectations. © 2026 IEEE.
