DO  - 10.1109/icdel65868.2025.11193607
AB  - With the advancement of massification in higher education, large-class teaching faces challenges such as heightened classroom management pressures, limited teacher-student interaction, and low student engagement. Grounded in the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM3), this study explores how a mobile terminal-based instant interactive system can enhance learning engagement in large-class settings. Employing an action research approach, a technological intervention was implemented in the Mental Health Education for College Students course at a Chinese university, with data analyzed through questionnaires and structural equation modeling (SEM). The findings reveal that perceived usefulness (PU) and perceived ease of use (PEOU) significantly and positively influence students' behavioral intention (BI) to adopt the technological tool, with PU exerting a more pronounced effect. Self-efficacy (SE) emerged as a key factor enhancing PEOU, while anxiety (ANX) exhibited a slight negative impact on PEOU. Furthermore, behavioral intention (BI) demonstrated a strong correlation with actual usage behavior (USE). The study proposes that strengthening course appeal, designing progressive tasks, and optimizing interactive features can further enhance students' technology acceptance. However, as the study solely focused on post-class data, future research should incorporate process-oriented data and multi-course validation to generalize the findings. The results offer practical insights for leveraging technology to empower large-class teaching and balancing scaled education with personalized cultivation. © 2025 IEEE.
