TI  - From Classroom to Entrepreneurship: An Analysis of Entrepreneurial Intention among Engineering Students at an Ecuadorian University
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 World Engineering Education Forum - Global Engineering Deans Council (WEEF-GEDC)
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/weef-gedc66748.2025.11256180
AB  - This study analyzes the factors influencing entrepreneurial intention among engineering students at an Ecuadorian university, applying Ajzen's Theory of Planned Behavior. Through a non-experimental quantitative approach, 254 students were surveyed using validated scales that assessed personal attitude, subjective norms, perceived behavioral control, entrepreneurial intention, and eight entrepreneurial competencies. The results reveal a favorable entrepreneurial profile characterized by positive attitudes and strong social support, but limited by low perception of practical self-efficacy. Perceived behavioral control obtained the lowest score, identified as the main barrier to materializing business initiatives. In entrepreneurial competencies, interpersonal skills predominate over technical-business skills. Significantly, no gender differences were found in entrepreneurial intention, challenging traditional stereotypes. The analysis revealed a statistically significant interaction effect between work experience and knowledge of entrepreneurs, demonstrating that these factors operate synergistically. The findings suggest the need to strengthen technical-business competencies and develop comprehensive educational strategies that combine practical experiences with exposure to successful entrepreneurial models to maximize entrepreneurial potential in engineering students. © 2025 IEEE.
N2  - This study analyzes the factors influencing entrepreneurial intention among engineering students at an Ecuadorian university, applying Ajzen's Theory of Planned Behavior. Through a non-experimental quantitative approach, 254 students were surveyed using validated scales that assessed personal attitude, subjective norms, perceived behavioral control, entrepreneurial intention, and eight entrepreneurial competencies. The results reveal a favorable entrepreneurial profile characterized by positive attitudes and strong social support, but limited by low perception of practical self-efficacy. Perceived behavioral control obtained the lowest score, identified as the main barrier to materializing business initiatives. In entrepreneurial competencies, interpersonal skills predominate over technical-business skills. Significantly, no gender differences were found in entrepreneurial intention, challenging traditional stereotypes. The analysis revealed a statistically significant interaction effect between work experience and knowledge of entrepreneurs, demonstrating that these factors operate synergistically. The findings suggest the need to strengthen technical-business competencies and develop comprehensive educational strategies that combine practical experiences with exposure to successful entrepreneurial models to maximize entrepreneurial potential in engineering students. © 2025 IEEE.
