TI  - Measuring Pedagogical Gaps in Cybersecurity Education: An LLM-Based Semantic Similarity Analysis with CyBOK
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Computing, ICOCO 2025
SP  - 118
EP  - 122
DO  - 10.1109/icoco67189.2025.11334156
AB  - The increasing complexity of cyber threats and rapid evolution of technologies demand that university cybersecurity curricula comprehensively align with industry-recognized frameworks, such as the Cyber Security Body of Knowledge (CyBOK). Traditional methods to evaluate curriculum coverage - such as manual syllabus reviews and expert panels - are often subjective, time-intensive, and challenging to scale. This paper proposes an automated, data-driven approach to measure the pedagogical gap between university curricula and CyBOK using transformer-based language models and cosine similarity. By encoding course descriptions and CyBOK Knowledge Areas with Sentence-BERT, we quantified semantic alignment and visualized coverage through a similarity heatmap. The results reveal consistent patterns: strong alignment with core technical areas, including Software Security and Network Security, but under-representation in specialized and emerging domains such as Formal Methods for Security, Cryptography, and Cyber-Physical Systems Security. This highlights the need for targeted curriculum updates to better address industry demands. The proposed method demonstrates the potential of natural language processing to support evidence-based curriculum design. Future work will incorporate qualitative expert validation and expand analysis to non-technical competencies and postgraduate programs. © 2025 IEEE.
