TI  - Assessing the Reliability of Artificial Intelligence (AI) when performing Process Calculations in an Undergraduate Chemical Engineering Course
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE Global Engineering Education Conference (EDUCON)
SP  - 1
EP  - 9
DO  - 10.1109/educon67543.2026.11574404
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) has radically shaped the current Fifth Industrial Revolution, with direct impacts on the skillsets perceived as desirable and/or valuable by Industry 5.0 (I.D. 5.0). This has consequently catalyzed curriculum revamps globally across Higher Education Institutes (HEIs), to address short-term Education 5.0 (E.D. 5.0) competencies, and the eventual long-term Society 5.0 vision. But another pernicious emerging problem is the easy (and typically free) access to Large-Language Models (LLMs) powered AI-based search platforms such as ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini, etc., which are often being used by students to create a more personalized experience (this includes solving questions, problem sets, tutorials, etc.). A complete revamp of the course "Introduction to Chemical & Biological Process Analysis"at the Department of Chemical & Biomolecular Engineering at Johns Hopkins University, with all tutorials/problem, sets sourced from a new database of material and energy balance questions, ranging in difficulty, differing from previous years. The instructor designed and solved these questions himself, and also fed them as search prompts to ChatGPT to elicit AI solutions/responses. These questions, presented weekly to a class of 47 freshmen/sophomore students, were solved and submitted weekly. The TAs graded these weekly reports based on a comprehensively designed rubric, following which, we gathered macro insights on the similarities/differences between student & AI responses. Information obtained presents direct comparative assessments between students/AI, while providing instructors with a detailed methodology and strategies to identify/avoid pitfalls. We envision this work to serve as an entry point towards investigating and analyzing which learning modalities (active/experiential/project-based/inquiry-driven, etc.) of learning act best within an AI-assisted learning framework at HEIs for undergraduates. More broadly, such studies serve to better understand LLMs and their exact contributions towards achieving Society 5.0. © 2026 IEEE.
