TI  - Learning Analytics for Dual Education: A Privacy-Preserving Framework Bridging Campus and Workplace Competencies
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Computational Intelligence Approaches and Applications, ICCIAA 2026 - Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/icciaa68481.2026.11543560
AB  - Dual education programs struggle with a fundamental challenge: demonstrating empirically how campus learning activities translate into workplace performance. This paper presents a privacy-preserving analytics framework that integrates learning management system behavioural patterns with supervisor-Assessed workplace competencies and an institutional outcomes assessment infrastructure. The architecture introduces three components: A behavioural indicator extraction engine operating on Moodle's event logs, a cross-context competency mapping protocol validated through expert consensus and aligned with institutional rubrics, and an uncertainty-Aware prediction pipeline with automated quality controls. Deployed across 47 students in computer science and software engineering programmes at the University of Petra over 12 weeks, the framework revealed strong associations (Spearman ρ = 0.61-0.76) between LMS engagement patterns and structured workplace evaluation rubrics measuring ten performance criteria. The system requires 15-20 hours of initial setup, operates under institutional review board-Approved governance protocols, and integrates with existing program learning outcomes assessment systems to provide comprehensive evidence for accreditation and quality assurance. © 2026 IEEE.
