TI  - Leveraging Generative AI for Creative Education: A Data-Driven Study of Design Creativity and Usability
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings - 2026 International Conference on Current Research in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, ICCRAIDS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/iccraids67816.2026.11519670
AB  - This study investigates the effectiveness of generative AI tools (MidJourney, DALL·E, and Adobe Firefly) in enhancing creativity and usability in visual communication design education. A quasi-experimental within-subject design was employed, evaluating the outputs of 30 undergraduate students through a multi-method analytical approach. Creativity was assessed using the Consensual Assessment Technique (CAT) by expert judges, while tool usability was measured via the System Usability Scale (SUS). To address the small hierarchical dataset and prevent data leakage, predictive analytics utilized a Random Forest regression model validated through a Leave-One-Subject-Out (LOSO) strategy. Results demonstrate that AI-assisted processes significantly outperform manual methods, yielding a large effect size (d=1.23). Within the AI ecosystem, MidJourney excelled in fostering visual originality, whereas Adobe Firefly achieved the highest usability ratings (M=82) due to its seamless workflow integration. Crucially, feature importance analysis revealed that quantified prompt specificity is the dominant predictor of creative success, accounting for 42% of the model's predictive weight with high mathematical accuracy (MAE = 0.32). These findings advocate for a 'Human-in-the-Loop' pedagogical paradigm, providing empirical evidence that elevating prompt engineering to a core cognitive competency is essential for curriculum design in the era of human-AI co-creation. © 2026 IEEE.
