DO  - 10.1109/iciet69664.2026.11561621
AB  - Outcome-based education defines the destination, competency-based education provides the skills to land there.' This research offers a richer, more systematic understanding of student resilience and cognition-based measurement within higher education. It signifies Adversity Quotient (AQ) and Bloom's Taxonomy (BT) together within an assessment framework. In higher education, students' cognitive levels are mapped with Bloom's assessment framework. In conventional academic-performance-based assessments, very little evidence is presented regarding students' psychological adaptability, resilience, and coping with adversity. The study investigates the correlation between the psychological trait (Adversity Quotient) and cognitive levels (Bloom's Taxonomy). A validated AQ tool was used to collect the adversity responses of 389 Second Year (SY) Engineering students. The AQ questionnaire is generated, validated, and used as the Adversity Response Profile for Indian Higher Education Institution (ARP-IHEI). AQ items are mapped to the Corpus and the SY question dataset, which comprises 600 questions. The cosine similarity metric and S-BERT were used to correlate AQ to Bloom's cognitive levels. Cosine similarity focuses on single-label classification, while combining it with S-BERT gives multilabel classification. By relating AQ evaluation to BT-based cognitive skills, educators can more easily discover a supportive yet inclusive learning environment. Students might need further guidance, emotional support, or the requirement to build skills. The study implies evidence-based tools usage to enhance students' mental health, learning, and career resilience for the future. © 2026 IEEE.
N2  - Outcome-based education defines the destination, competency-based education provides the skills to land there.' This research offers a richer, more systematic understanding of student resilience and cognition-based measurement within higher education. It signifies Adversity Quotient (AQ) and Bloom's Taxonomy (BT) together within an assessment framework. In higher education, students' cognitive levels are mapped with Bloom's assessment framework. In conventional academic-performance-based assessments, very little evidence is presented regarding students' psychological adaptability, resilience, and coping with adversity. The study investigates the correlation between the psychological trait (Adversity Quotient) and cognitive levels (Bloom's Taxonomy). A validated AQ tool was used to collect the adversity responses of 389 Second Year (SY) Engineering students. The AQ questionnaire is generated, validated, and used as the Adversity Response Profile for Indian Higher Education Institution (ARP-IHEI). AQ items are mapped to the Corpus and the SY question dataset, which comprises 600 questions. The cosine similarity metric and S-BERT were used to correlate AQ to Bloom's cognitive levels. Cosine similarity focuses on single-label classification, while combining it with S-BERT gives multilabel classification. By relating AQ evaluation to BT-based cognitive skills, educators can more easily discover a supportive yet inclusive learning environment. Students might need further guidance, emotional support, or the requirement to build skills. The study implies evidence-based tools usage to enhance students' mental health, learning, and career resilience for the future. © 2026 IEEE.
