TI  - Quantitative Intensive for the not so Mathematically Minded: Project Based Research in Agricultural Systems
PY  - 2023
Y1  - 2023
T2  - 2023 ASABE Annual International Meeting
DO  - 10.13031/aim.202301486
AB  - Project-based learning (PBL) theory transverses STEM and Non-STEM disciplines and often is utilized abundantly in engineering and applied technology courses. This presentation will highlight a student-faculty collaboratively PBL research activity within an agricultural structures and environments course to enhance student quantitative skill development program. Collaborative efforts between participants sought to scaffold instruction to assist students in making meaningful connections to promote a systems approach to problem-solving. The objective of this approach was to facilitate student-driven learning activities and the application of critical thinking in realistic situations. A group research project was assigned with a corresponding content unit associated with the course. Options included metal building for hay storage, wooden structure (pole-barn) for equipment storage, or concrete storage structure for dry manure. The project involved establishing design objectives, the collection/validation of data, the analysis of data, and the interpretation of data for structural recommendations. Emphasizing mathematical calculations related to wood and metal products, wood and metal construction, and various calculations related to constructing and maintaining agricultural structures. Students were assigned groups to complete the project. Instructional strategies included demonstrations with models, utilization of student-feedback rubrics, skill acquisition activities, and dialogue to develop students' reflection. Students organize their research and applied content knowledge to complete a poster presentation program. This paper highlights this approach to quantitative skill development at Utah State University. © 2023 ASABE Annual International Meeting. All Rights Reserved.
