AB  - All working environments require engineering graduates to have both hard and soft skills to optimally perform their work. The working environment requires graduates to have “Work Enhancement Skills (WES)” as a soft skills, where WES is a combination of the three soft skills namely adaptability, flexibility and the ability to work without supervision / dependability. This paper deals with a study to assess the perception of students and faculty with regard to opportunities available and its utilization for the students to develop their WES. Two research instruments, one for students and the other for faculty, with a five point Liker scale and five items were created; their reliability and content validity were ensured and used to collect facts. The facts thus gathered was put to two tests (both item wise and dimension wise)with the software “Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS)”, the tests being independent sample t-test and paired sample t-test. Inferences and conclusions of the study have been presented. © TJPRC Pvt. Ltd.
N2  - All working environments require engineering graduates to have both hard and soft skills to optimally perform their work. The working environment requires graduates to have “Work Enhancement Skills (WES)” as a soft skills, where WES is a combination of the three soft skills namely adaptability, flexibility and the ability to work without supervision / dependability. This paper deals with a study to assess the perception of students and faculty with regard to opportunities available and its utilization for the students to develop their WES. Two research instruments, one for students and the other for faculty, with a five point Liker scale and five items were created; their reliability and content validity were ensured and used to collect facts. The facts thus gathered was put to two tests (both item wise and dimension wise)with the software “Statistical Package for Social Sciences (SPSS)”, the tests being independent sample t-test and paired sample t-test. Inferences and conclusions of the study have been presented. © TJPRC Pvt. Ltd.
