TI  - Adoption and Implementation of Artificial Intelligence in Education: Insights from Science Teachers
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - 2025 11th International Conference on Education and Technology: Personalized AI for Digital Well-being: Bridging Technology and Humanity, ICET 2025
SP  - 259
EP  - 262
DO  - 10.1109/icet67257.2025.11290822
AB  - This study examined the relationship between the level of artificial intelligence (AI) adoption and its implementation in science education. A descriptive-correlational research design was employed, involving 55 science teachers from three purposively selected public secondary schools in Butuan City, Agusan del Norte, Philippines. Participants were chosen through simple random sampling, and data were collected using a researcher-adapted and content-validated questionnaire with strong reliability (Cronbach's alpha = 0.80). The instrument measured AI adoption dimensions, awareness, and readiness, institutional support, professional development, technological resources, and their correlation with AI implementation in instruction, assessment, and student engagement. Results analyzed using the Spearman Rank-Order Correlation Coefficient revealed strong and statistically significant positive associations across all dimensions. The overall level of AI adoption (ρ = 0.858, p <.001) emerged as the strongest predictor of implementation success, underscoring that effective AI integration requires access to resources, teacher preparedness, institutional commitment, and supportive leadership. These findings affirm that AI adoption is a multifactorial process, rooted in individual capacity and systemic support, and directly linked to improved teaching effectiveness, enhanced student outcomes, and greater instructional productivity. The study contributes empirical evidence to the growing literature on AI in Education, particularly within the Philippine context. It suggests that policymakers, school leaders, and teacher education programs should strategically invest in digital competencies, equitable access to AI tools, and continuous professional development to foster sustainable and inclusive AI integration in schools. © 2025 IEEE.
