TI  - Construction and Empirical Study of a Project-Driven Online-Offline Blended Learning Model by Linear Regression, Multiple Regression, and Principal Component Analysis
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of The 2nd International Conference on Intelligent Education and Computer Technology, IECT 2025
SP  - 377
EP  - 382
DO  - 10.1145/3764206.3764264
AB  - In response to the increasing demand for interdisciplinary, practice-oriented, and technology-enhanced learning under the Educational Informatization 2.0 Action Plan and the New Engineering Education framework, this study constructs a blended learning model based on “Project-Driven × Interdisciplinary Integration × Intelligent Support.” The model applies to a Systems Analysis course for information management undergraduates. The purpose of this research is to assess the model's effectiveness in enhancing students' multidimensional competencies and to explore its underlying mechanisms. A structured questionnaire targets 300 students majoring in information management, covering course satisfaction, perceived competency improvement, and platform utilization. Quantitative analysis adopts linear regression, multiple linear regression, and principal component analysis (PCA) to examine the influence of instructional variables on competency development. Findings show that the model improves core competencies such as teamwork, knowledge application, innovation, communication, and problem-solving. Course satisfaction and platform support consistently serve as the most influential predictors across all models, with the multiple linear regression model presenting the highest explanatory power. PCA identifies a latent general competency factor (PC1), which accounts for over 80% of the variance, further supporting the model's validity. This study offers empirical support for micro-level teaching reform and contributes to the theoretical understanding of interdisciplinary talent cultivation. The model exhibits strong adaptability and scalability, providing a practical framework for curriculum innovation in engineering education. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
