SP  - 17
EP  - 22
DO  - 10.1109/aieta69357.2026.11508664
AB  - The ability growth prediction of teaching practice students is a key issue to improve the quality of rural teachers' training. Based on 1,247 normal students and 42 million teaching interaction logs generated during the 18-week teaching period, this study constructs a prediction model of ability growth and identifies key influencing factors. The study developed a competency framework spanning three dimensions-instructional implementation, technology integration, and professional development-and employed latent class growth analysis to uncover four heterogeneous trajectories: steady growth, rapid ascent, plateau-fluctuation, and persistent low. A gradient boosting prediction model was subsequently built, achieving an R2 of 0.72 under five-fold cross-validation. Findings revealed that technology-enhanced behavioral markers, notably the interaction depth index during weeks 3-4 and the consistency of reflective practice, served as robust predictors of later competence development. Conversely, conventional preparation activities such as resource downloads showed negative predictive value. The research shows that the early teaching interaction log can realize the risk early warning and type identification of ability growth, which provides the method support for the data-driven teacher education decision. © 2026 IEEE.
