SP  - 1
EP  - 8
DO  - 10.1109/educon67543.2026.11574323
AB  - Creativity is one of the competences most frequently advocated for the XXI century. Students’ creativity in informatics education is typically challenged when constructing programs with block-based languages, such as ScratchJr. The typical task for the ScratchJr language is to develop an open-ended program, which usually is a story of the theme chosen by the student. Therefore, students must show both their programming proficiency and their creativity with the story. The paper presents a new measurement instrument of creativity in ScratchJr programs. The instrument instantiates the dimensions of Torrance's definition of creativity (namely, fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration) in terms of the program elements and themes. The instrument was evaluated with programs developed by teachers in training for Early Childhood and Primary Education. A pilot evaluation was first conducted with a small number of programs of varying degrees of complexity. Later, the instrument was jointly applied and refined by three instructors to achieve construct validity. The paper presents the instrument, illustrated with examples. The main implication of the instrument presented is that not only ScratchJr programs can be assessed objectively on the student mastery of programming (usually by means of rubrics) but also on the apparently subjective aspect of creativity.
