T2  - IEEE Transactions on Signal and Power Integrity
VL  - 5
SP  - 123
EP  - 131
DO  - 10.1109/tsipi.2026.3708791
AB  - This work integrates the sixth-order Gaussian kernel least mean algorithm (SOGKLMA) and an optimized fractional order proportional–integral–derivative (FOPID) controller to enhance the electrical power quality (EPQ). To address the EPQ issues, the first key objective is to extract the fundamental quantity from the disturbed grid by employing (SOGKLMA). An SOGKLMA enhances dynamic performance through higher order filtering that effectively adapts to excess errors by incorporating a Gaussian kernel function. This accurately estimates the fundamental component to maintain the load voltage at its predefined value, thereby estimating reference load voltage generation and enabling faster convergence under severe transients. Moreover, an FOPID controller is employed to maintain the DC-link voltage at 300 V. The effectiveness of FOPID is validated by integrating the objective function, integral time square error (ITSE), with a comparative study. The artificial protozoa optimizer (APO)-based FOPID confirms superior performance compared with the recently developed Beluga whale optimizer (BWO), yielding a lower ITSE of 0.001528 compared with 0.044899. The simulation responses of the proposed APO-tuned FOPID ensure its supremacy by comparing with a recently developed approach called the BWO with an enhanced transient response, including lessening the maximum overshoot (Mp = 5.7%), undershoot (Us = 3.44%), settling time (Ts = 0.15s), and steady-state (ess = 0.678%). Finally, system robustness is examined through experimentation with a laboratory-scaled-down DVR prototype.
N2  - This work integrates the sixth-order Gaussian kernel least mean algorithm (SOGKLMA) and an optimized fractional order proportional–integral–derivative (FOPID) controller to enhance the electrical power quality (EPQ). To address the EPQ issues, the first key objective is to extract the fundamental quantity from the disturbed grid by employing (SOGKLMA). An SOGKLMA enhances dynamic performance through higher order filtering that effectively adapts to excess errors by incorporating a Gaussian kernel function. This accurately estimates the fundamental component to maintain the load voltage at its predefined value, thereby estimating reference load voltage generation and enabling faster convergence under severe transients. Moreover, an FOPID controller is employed to maintain the DC-link voltage at 300 V. The effectiveness of FOPID is validated by integrating the objective function, integral time square error (ITSE), with a comparative study. The artificial protozoa optimizer (APO)-based FOPID confirms superior performance compared with the recently developed Beluga whale optimizer (BWO), yielding a lower ITSE of 0.001528 compared with 0.044899. The simulation responses of the proposed APO-tuned FOPID ensure its supremacy by comparing with a recently developed approach called the BWO with an enhanced transient response, including lessening the maximum overshoot (Mp = 5.7%), undershoot (Us = 3.44%), settling time (Ts = 0.15s), and steady-state (ess = 0.678%). Finally, system robustness is examined through experimentation with a laboratory-scaled-down DVR prototype.
