T2  - 2025 IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE)
SP  - 1
EP  - 5
DO  - 10.1109/fie63693.2025.11328273
AB  - This Work-in-Progress innovative practice paper presents curriculum aimed at integrating microelectronics content into a first-year engineering design course. This paper focuses on the impact of integrating microelectronics into a first-year engineering design course that reaches over 2,500 engineering students each year from all schools of engineering. The course instruction is grounded in engineering design problems that utilize microelectronics activities to teach data analytics, modeling, and engineering design. We present three microelectronics design projects designed to scaffold students in designing and programming with microcontrollers. The impact of the microelectronics activities was assessed using a retrospective pre/post survey measuring interest, motivation, awareness, and transformative experiences. The results of the quantitative analysis revealed that students demonstrated an increase in interest, engagement, and self-efficacy surrounding microelectronics. This research contributes insights into how firstyear engineering students experience integrated microelectronics activities. Findings from this study offer guidance for optimizing early educational interventions that are aimed at strengthening the microelectronics workforce. By fostering interest and reducing barriers to understanding and using microelectronics, introductory engineering courses can play a vital role in addressing the skills and employment gap in the semiconductor industry and ensuring a robust pipeline of future engineers.
N2  - This Work-in-Progress innovative practice paper presents curriculum aimed at integrating microelectronics content into a first-year engineering design course. This paper focuses on the impact of integrating microelectronics into a first-year engineering design course that reaches over 2,500 engineering students each year from all schools of engineering. The course instruction is grounded in engineering design problems that utilize microelectronics activities to teach data analytics, modeling, and engineering design. We present three microelectronics design projects designed to scaffold students in designing and programming with microcontrollers. The impact of the microelectronics activities was assessed using a retrospective pre/post survey measuring interest, motivation, awareness, and transformative experiences. The results of the quantitative analysis revealed that students demonstrated an increase in interest, engagement, and self-efficacy surrounding microelectronics. This research contributes insights into how firstyear engineering students experience integrated microelectronics activities. Findings from this study offer guidance for optimizing early educational interventions that are aimed at strengthening the microelectronics workforce. By fostering interest and reducing barriers to understanding and using microelectronics, introductory engineering courses can play a vital role in addressing the skills and employment gap in the semiconductor industry and ensuring a robust pipeline of future engineers.
