TI  - AI-Based Adaptive Routing Protocols for IoT Wireless Sensor Networks
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 1st International Conference on Advancement in Futuristic Technologies (ICAFT)
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/icaft66710.2025.11452979
AB  - The significant rise in Internet of Things (IoT) application has compounded the implementation of Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) where efficient routing has been a thorn in the flesh because of limited energy, dense node population, and variable traffic environment. Traditional routing algorithms like LEACH and RPL offer base line solutions but usually cannot adjust to atypical energy distributions and dynamic changing topologies and results in untimely node demise and poor network competence. In this paper, an AI-based adaptive routing framework is proposed, which combines reinforcement learning in the dynamic choice of routes, fuzzy logic in energy-optimal decision-making, and deep neural networks in predicting the load of traffic. The model was applied in NS-3 where a network of 200 nodes was used on a 500 m x 500 m field, and the nodes initially started with an energy of 2 J each. The experimental findings indicated that the proposed protocol extended the network lifetime by 390 rounds more compared to LEACH, enhanced the ratio of packet delivery by 13 points, and decreased the average end to end delay by 176 ms in comparison with 242 ms. Such findings indicate that AI-based adaptivity supports a greater energy efficiency but also results in better reliability and responsiveness, which is why the method is appropriate in large-scale IoT applications.
