AB  - This study aims to demonstrate the design of a digital test application database based on the Matundan concept. The Borg and Gall model is used as a development model in this study. The development stages referring to the Borg and Gall model in this 2025 study only focus on the development stage of the database design, initial trials, and revisions to the results of the initial trials. The subjects involved in obtaining data from the initial trials of the digital test application database design based on the Matundan concept were 42 respondents. These respondents included: one informatics expert, one education expert, and the assessment of 10 lecturers and 30 students. The data collection tool for the initial trials was a questionnaire. The data analysis technique for this study was by comparing the results of the initial trials of the digital test application database design based on the Matundan concept with quality standards based on a five scale. The results of this study demonstrate the quality of the digital test application database design based on the Matundan concept falls into the good category. The impact of this research is the transformation of conventional assessment tools into digital assessments as a form of innovation in evaluation research, the existence of an operational framework that maps each Matundan stage into the specific difficulty level of each question item in the digital test application, and the existence of a track record of student development from basic to advanced levels with neatly stored data details.
