TI  - Developing Leadership Skills in Software Engineering Students - a Case Study of a Leadership Development Framework
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE Frontiers in Education Conference (FIE)
SP  - 1
EP  - 9
DO  - 10.1109/fie63693.2025.11328306
AB  - This Research-to-Practice full paper evaluates the impact of a leadership development framework within a Mobile Application Development (MAD) course, focusing on the integration of Scrum, Challenge-Based Learning (CBL), and shared leadership. The study addresses the growing importance of soft skills in software engineering education and aims to provide insights into the influence of this framework on the leadership skills of software engineering students. The framework combined agile practices, specifically Scrum, with CBL and shared leadership, incorporating “role rotation” in which student teams rotated Scrum roles (Scrum Master and Product Owner) at the end of each sprint. An 8-week case study was conducted with nine teams, four of which applied the framework. After each sprint, all team members completed a questionnaire to assess their teamwork, with a final Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) questionnaire administered at the end of the development cycle. The results showed that the framework was easy to adopt, improved team collaboration, and empowered students to take proactive leadership roles. Although the generation of CBL guiding questions had less impact than anticipated, the overall structure supported collaborative learning. Some students suggested that the role rotation ceremony could be shortened to better align with Scrum Sprint Retrospective outcomes. These findings provide valuable insights for academia and industry. In academia, the framework offers a way to enhance software engineering education by integrating leadership skills development into Scrum-based group projects. For industry, the framework can foster improved team cohesion by reframing leadership as a collaborative process.
N2  - This Research-to-Practice full paper evaluates the impact of a leadership development framework within a Mobile Application Development (MAD) course, focusing on the integration of Scrum, Challenge-Based Learning (CBL), and shared leadership. The study addresses the growing importance of soft skills in software engineering education and aims to provide insights into the influence of this framework on the leadership skills of software engineering students. The framework combined agile practices, specifically Scrum, with CBL and shared leadership, incorporating “role rotation” in which student teams rotated Scrum roles (Scrum Master and Product Owner) at the end of each sprint. An 8-week case study was conducted with nine teams, four of which applied the framework. After each sprint, all team members completed a questionnaire to assess their teamwork, with a final Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) questionnaire administered at the end of the development cycle. The results showed that the framework was easy to adopt, improved team collaboration, and empowered students to take proactive leadership roles. Although the generation of CBL guiding questions had less impact than anticipated, the overall structure supported collaborative learning. Some students suggested that the role rotation ceremony could be shortened to better align with Scrum Sprint Retrospective outcomes. These findings provide valuable insights for academia and industry. In academia, the framework offers a way to enhance software engineering education by integrating leadership skills development into Scrum-based group projects. For industry, the framework can foster improved team cohesion by reframing leadership as a collaborative process.
