TI  - Psychological and Behavioral Implications of Intellectual Humility
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 World Engineering Education Forum - Global Engineering Deans Council (WEEF-GEDC)
SP  - 1
EP  - 7
DO  - 10.1109/weef-gedc66748.2025.11256293
AB  - Intellectual humility (IH), broadly defined as the recognition and acceptance of one's cognitive limitations and the willingness to revise beliefs in light of new evidence, has garnered increasing attention within psychological science for its potential role in shaping epistemic, social, and behavioral outcomes. Though historically rooted in philosophical discourse, IH has evolved into a distinctive psychological construct, encompassing cognitive, affective, and behavioral dimensions that hold considerable relevance for individual functioning and group dynamics. This study integrates a comprehensive theoretical synthesis with empirical investigation to elucidate the psychological architecture of IH and explore its practical implications, particularly within academic and professional contexts. The research employed a dual-method approach: a critical review of interdisciplinary literature including cognitive psychology, personality theory, and virtue epistemology to map the conceptual terrain of IH, followed by an empirical component comprising quantitative data $(\mathrmN=40)$ gathered through validated IH measures and qualitative insights from semi-structured interviews ($\mathrmn=40$). Statistical analyses, including structural equation modelling and correlational assessments, revealed that IH is positively associated with openness to experience and perspective-taking, while inversely correlated with dogmatism and overconfidence. Exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses confirmed a multidimensional framework, integrating elements of intellectual modesty, awareness of intellectual fallibility, and willingness to engage with dissenting views. The results underscore the value of cultivating IH through targeted interventions in educational and organizational settings, where it may foster ethical reasoning, epistemic responsibility, and constructive dialogue across ideological divides. This research provides a foundational step toward the operationalization of IH as a measurable and malleable trait with implications for enhancing individual selfregulation and promoting collective intelligence
