DO  - 10.1109/vlsitsa64674.2025.11046805
AB  - In real-time EEG neural seizures detection, the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) algorithms are applied to perform time-frequency transformation before classification. This paper presents a 16-bit 64-2048-point FFT Application Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) tailored for efficient, lowpower electroencephalography (EEG) signal processing. The pipeline structure ensures the non-stop computation of EEG signals from different channels in time division, achieving realtime processing with flexible architectures, reusable twiddles, and low power clock gating techniques. The design accommodates configurable 64-2048-point FFT processing. The chip was fabricated using a 130nm CMOS process and a minimalist five-metal layer stack to reduce manufacturing costs. The chip exhibits robustness in a wide range of working frequency and power supply ($100 ~\mathrmK ∼ 20 \textMHz, 0.9 ∼ 1.5 ~\mathrmV$). The proposed design demonstrates superior energy efficiency and lower overall cost compared to contemporary FFT chip implementations. Its efficacy is validated through highly accurate EEG processing results, thereby confirming the design's competence.
N2  - In real-time EEG neural seizures detection, the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) algorithms are applied to perform time-frequency transformation before classification. This paper presents a 16-bit 64-2048-point FFT Application Specific Integrated Circuit (ASIC) tailored for efficient, lowpower electroencephalography (EEG) signal processing. The pipeline structure ensures the non-stop computation of EEG signals from different channels in time division, achieving realtime processing with flexible architectures, reusable twiddles, and low power clock gating techniques. The design accommodates configurable 64-2048-point FFT processing. The chip was fabricated using a 130nm CMOS process and a minimalist five-metal layer stack to reduce manufacturing costs. The chip exhibits robustness in a wide range of working frequency and power supply ($100 ~\mathrmK ∼ 20 \textMHz, 0.9 ∼ 1.5 ~\mathrmV$). The proposed design demonstrates superior energy efficiency and lower overall cost compared to contemporary FFT chip implementations. Its efficacy is validated through highly accurate EEG processing results, thereby confirming the design's competence.
