TI  - Artificial Intelligence Literacy and Perception of Employment Prospects Among Final-Year Undergraduates
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Computer, Data Sciences and Applications (ACDSA)
SP  - 1
EP  - 6
DO  - 10.1109/acdsa67686.2026.11468141
AB  - In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI) has taken center stage in our lives. It has significantly disrupted traditional industries and labor markets, presenting both opportunities and challenges in the workplace. This development motivates this research to study the uncertainty and anxieties about the future work associated with AI's influence on employment prospects, with the focus on final-year undergraduates. A sequential mixed-methods approach was applied with a qualitative interview followed by a quantitative survey. Thematic analysis was performed to identify the themes, while structural equation modeling (SEM) was applied to test the conceptual model as a pilot test before expanding the study to a larger scale of respondents. Overall, findings indicate that the undergraduates are aware of the AI concepts and tools with a mix of excitement and anxiety about the future workplace. The pilot test results show that the identified constructs exhibit reliability and validity, showing potential relationships on the path proposed in the conceptual framework, and the respondents felt positive about AI in enhancing employment prospects, yet expressed simultaneous uncertainty about job security.
