TI  - AUTOMATED ASSESSMENT AND PEER EVALUATION OF UNIVERSITY STUDENTS USING ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE TOOLS
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 19th IADIS International Conference Information Systems 2026 and 24th International Conference on e-Society, IS ES 2026
SP  - 369
EP  - 376
AB  - The article presents a comprehensive and methodologically grounded analysis of automated assessment and peer assessment practices in higher education facilitated by artificial intelligence (AI) tools. The research methodology is based on a systematic review of scholarly sources published between 2020 and 2025, a comparative analysis of leading international digital assessment platforms (Gradescope, PeerScholar, Peerceptiv, EvaluMate, RiPPLE), and an empirical survey of academic staff at Ukrainian higher education institutions. Primary empirical data were collected through an online questionnaire administered to 204 university teachers representing diverse levels of teaching experience (up to 5 years - 14%, 5-10 years - 32%, more than 10 years - 54%). The findings indicate a low level of practical adoption of AI-based assessment platforms: only 21.6% of respondents reported prior experience with such tools, while over 50% encountered them for the first time during the survey. Among the most frequently mentioned platforms were Grammarly (19.5%), Gradescope (13.7%), PeerScholar (18%), and Peergrade (13.2%); however, their regular and systematic use was reported by only 1-5% of participants. Respondents identified time efficiency (69.3%), enhanced quality of feedback (35.1%), and increased opportunities for collaborative learning (30.7%) as the principal advantages of AI-supported assessment. In contrast, the contribution of these tools to the development of students' critical thinking skills was acknowledged less frequently (18.4%). The main perceived limitations included technical constraints (44%), insufficient student motivation (40.4%), interface usability issues (23.9%), and concerns regarding the fairness and transparency of assessment outcomes (20.2%). A qualitative analysis revealed statistically and pedagogically significant differences in the perception of AI-based assessment depending on teaching experience. Educators with 5-10 years of experience more often conceptualize AI platforms as instruments for enhancing peer interaction and collaborative learning processes, whereas more experienced teachers (over 10 years) tend to evaluate these technologies primarily in terms of efficiency, feedback quality, and assessment objectivity. The findings further suggest that a higher level of digital teaching integration is associated with a more critical and reflective evaluation of the pedagogical appropriateness of AI-driven assessment solutions. Overall, the results align with prevailing international trends while simultaneously exposing the fragmented, sporadic, and largely unsystematic nature of AI tool implementation in Ukrainian higher education assessment practices. The study substantiates the necessity of developing a coherent national strategy for the digital transformation of assessment, strengthening teachers' digital and assessment literacy, and ensuring the ethical robustness, transparency, and reliability of AI systems used in evaluating academic achievement. © ISES 2026.
