TI  - Emerging Approaches in Computing Education: AI-Powered Heutagogy for Sustainable and Self-Directed Learning
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 International Conference on Future Technologies (ICFT)
SP  - 1
EP  - 8
DO  - 10.1109/icft66708.2025.11336596
AB  - This paper proposes an Artificial Intelligence (AI)-Driven Heutagogy Framework custom-designed for engineering education with intentional alignment to Sustainable Development Goal 4 and sustainable software practices. The framework consists of: (1) an AI-Assessment Engine that automates multi-level evaluations according to Revised Bloom's Taxonomy with supervised learning algorithms and rubric-based scores; (2) an Automated E-Learning System providing AI-driven, gamified learning trajectories with adaptive neural architectures; and (3) a Metacognitive Layer utilizing NLP-based sentiment and creativity detection with reflective journals. In an empirical case study with 120 engineering students (AI-assisted vs. non-AI groups), they created real-time image classification CNN models. The AI-assisted cohort produced a 133% increment in model iterations $(28± 3\ \textvs.\ 12± 2)$, 53% decrease in debugging time (1.5 vs. 3.2 hours), and 42% increase in RBT-based rubric scores $(9.2± 1.1\ \textvs.\ 6.5± 1.5)$ • Moreover, their models exhibited a 42% higher inference speed (135 vs. 95 frames per second), 29% reduced memory usage (220 vs. 310 MB), and higher classification accuracy (F1-score: $0.92± 0.03$ vs. $0.85± 0.04)$. Nonetheless, their peer-reviewed creativity ratings were 15% lower (7.1 vs. 8.3/10), which is a signal to curb algorithmic dependency. Critical incident technique-based qualitative analysis found that the AI cohort attained 35% higher metacognitive depth and facilitated a 60% reduction in grading workloads for educators. To counter creativity risks, the framework incorporates randomized prompt modules and reflective checkpoints. These findings validate AI-based heutagogy's scalability to efficiently upgrade engineering curricula and assessment to meet SDG 4 with open-source tool deployment and policy initiatives toward balanced technical and creativity development.
