AB  - In general, students in engineering institutions are less proficient in the acquisition of English. Based on self-reported difficulty levels in speaking, writing, and comprehension tasks on a 5-point Likert scale, it has been observed that second-year engineering students encounter 30-35% more challenges in English communication than first-year students. This paper introduces a questionnaire that is well-organised and includes constructs that are pertinent to the efficacy of teaching and the factors that impede the acquisition of the English language. In order to evaluate the efficacy of English instruction and the obstacles that impede student learning in engineering institutions, a comparative analysis is implemented. The study applied Cronbach’s Alpha EFA PCA and KMO and Bartlett tests on the constructs of the questionnaire to check the data and draw necessary insights. From the thorough study, it can be concluded that students desire to have more interactive and digital learning methods, specifically asynchronous learning platforms, AI-driven pronunciation tools, and gamified language modules. They also want lessons that connect to major recruiters in present scenario. Most of the students are willing to take on more work to get a better education. With this approach, schools shall be able to make better English classes for engineering students. Teachers will be able to read their students' minds and cater their lessons to their preferred mediums, whether that's digital resources or teaching them marketable skills. With this data in hand, educational institutions may refine their pedagogical approaches, leading to higher quality student achievement.
