TI  - Benchmarking Large Language Models on Power System Logic: The "Five Preventions" Challenge for Safety-Critical Physical Reasoning
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
T2  - 2026 3rd International Conference on Power System Engineering and Smart Grid (PSESG)
SP  - 358
EP  - 361
DO  - 10.1109/psesg69490.2026.11544817
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong logical capabilities in textual domains, yet their reliability in safety-critical cyber-physical control remains insufficiently characterized. In power system switching, a single logically invalid command can trigger catastrophic equipment and personnel hazards. We introduce PowerLogic-Bench, a benchmark grounded in the "Five Preventions" interlocking constraints aligned with IEC 61850 CILO logic. Unlike static QA-style tests, we formulate reasoning as non-monotonic planning over dynamic Text-Attributed Graphs (TAGs), with safety constraints formalized in First-Order Logic (FOL). Using programmatic generation on IEEE test topologies, we construct 15,000 deterministic scenarios and evaluate 14 representative LLMs. Results reveal a consistent "Compliance-Competence Divergence": models often complete operational goals while violating hard safety constraints, especially in long-horizon tasks. We further identify an "Inverted-U" scaling law where mid-scale models exhibit peak hazard rates, and demonstrate that neuro-symbolic feedback mitigates but does not eliminate residual risks. Our findings indicate that standalone LLM policies are insufficient for autonomous safety-critical switching and should be paired with symbolic verification layers.
