s had not used the assessment system to innovate. It is contended that innovation is unlikely in the absence of substantial prior knowledge that can enable an innovation to be progressed. It is similarly contended that the friction caused by the ABET proposals cannot be resolved without attention to knowledge that exists in the area of accreditation and assessment. The purpose of this paper is to review some of that research and scholarship as it relates to the proposition that university courses should produce graduates who are able to take up positions in industry without further training.
