TI  - Proposed model for studying the factors affecting customer satisfaction on service quality of logistics companies in Bac Ninh province
PY  - 2025
Y1  - 2025
T2  - 2025 Asia Meeting on Environment and Electrical Engineering (EEE-AM)
SP  - 1
EP  - 5
DO  - 10.1109/eee-am66675.2025.11473769
AB  - Amid rapid industrialization in Vietnam’s Red River Delta, the logistics sector in Bac Ninh Province plays a vital role in enhancing supply chain efficiency and export competitiveness. However, growing market competition compels local providers to strengthen service quality to sustain customer loyalty. This study identifies key determinants of customer satisfaction with logistics services using an extended SERVQUAL framework. A quantitative survey of 120 logistics customers in Bac Ninh Province was conducted on a 5-point Likert scale. Exploratory factor analysis (EFA) and multiple linear regression (MLR) were applied to validate the model. The results show that workforce competence and timeliness are the most influential factors, followed by infrastructure and technology, adaptability and innovation, and pricing. The findings provide empirical evidence and managerial implications for improving service quality and competitiveness among regional logistics firms.
N2  - Amid rapid industrialization in Vietnam’s Red River Delta, the logistics sector in Bac Ninh Province plays a vital role in enhancing supply chain efficiency and export competitiveness. However, growing market competition compels local providers to strengthen service quality to sustain customer loyalty. This study identifies key determinants of customer satisfaction with logistics services using an extended SERVQUAL framework. A quantitative survey of 120 logistics customers in Bac Ninh Province was conducted on a 5-point Likert scale. Exploratory factor analysis (EFA) and multiple linear regression (MLR) were applied to validate the model. The results show that workforce competence and timeliness are the most influential factors, followed by infrastructure and technology, adaptability and innovation, and pricing. The findings provide empirical evidence and managerial implications for improving service quality and competitiveness among regional logistics firms.
