TI  - Generative AI as a Catalyst for Innovation and Teaching Improvements: Challenges and Opportunities
PY  - 2026
Y1  - 2026
JO  - Ethical Challenges of AI in Academia: Impact and Implications for Higher Education
T2  - Ethical Challenges of AI in Academia: Impact and Implications for Higher Education
SP  - 147
EP  - 168
AB  - In the current era, we find ourselves navigating increasingly blurred boundaries between human and artificial intelligence (AI), paving the way to that which has come to be known as ‘precision education’ (Olmedo-Moreno et al., 2024). This paradigm emphasises personalisation in higher education, wherein generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) minimises the time allocated to mechanical tasks, thereby allowing greater focus on essential cognitive processes such as creativity and critical thinking. However, significant concerns have arisen within educational institutions regarding the implementation of AI. UNESCO (2023) and the European Union (2023) underscore the need to regulate AI, given its ethical implications, as evidenced by the recent proposal put forth in the EU Artificial Intelligence Act. This legislation aims to ensure that AI systems operational within the EU are both safe and respectful of fundamental rights by adopting a regulatory framework that is predicated on risk assessment. This law delineates four levels of risk, which are assessed on a scale ranging from unacceptable to low. Each level is subjected to regulations that are tailored towards their specific characteristics. Notably, educational or professional training is deemed a critical criterion with the absence of such training leading to the determination of unacceptable risk. In response to such regulatory frameworks, universities have formulated guidelines promoting best practice for AI use in both teaching and research. These guidelines advocate for the ethical and safe application of AI, fostering a reflective and pragmatic approach to learning. The integration of AI in higher education, not only, simplifies the personalisation of learning tailored towards individual needs, but, also, enhances the efficiency and accuracy of data collection and analysis, thereby facilitating more informed educational decisions. Nevertheless, AI integration is not devoid of challenges, such as the need to transform learning strategies and deliver comprehensive training to educators so that they adeptly wield AI tools. Artificial intelligence is revolutionising teaching within higher education by refining curriculum planning and pedagogical methods to better meet the educational demands and personal and professional skills of students. This methodological shift allows for the development and enhancement of educational guides that incorporate new competencies such as digital literacy. Nonetheless, the deployment of AI is accompanied by challenges, including inconsistencies in its application and the need to establish standardised procedures that effectively integrate AI elements within educational materials (Expósito and Olmedo, 2024). Moreover, AI advocates for dynamic and participatory teaching methods, both in physical and virtual settings, facilitated by the universal design for learning and elaboration of adaptable educational materials. However, the implementation of such active teaching methodologies necessitates the continuous professional development of educators and students and introduces risks such as privacy breaches and identity fraud, which brings about a need to use tools such as ChatGPT and reliable ‘fact-checking’ tools. In the context of tutoring, GenAI aids in pinpointing learning deficits and crafting personalised learning activities for students with specific needs. Effective integration of GenAI into this process hinges on the proper training of educators and availability of suitable infrastructure. In terms of assessment, GenAI refines evaluation methodologies, enabling deeper critical reflection and the customisation of evaluative materials. Although GenAI enhances both the correction and self-correction of assessments, it is crucial that the authenticity and validity of GenAI content is verified and that it is ensured that final evaluative decisions remain within the purview of educators. Collectively, AI alleviates the mechanical burden associated with teaching and promotes a more innovative and personalised educational approach that is tailored to meet the individual needs of students and is congruent with hybrid learning models. While it is true that GenAI furnishes potent tools for customising and enhancing educational outcomes, a methodical and regulated approach is required to maximise its benefits and mitigate potential risks. The ongoing adaptation of educators and students to the nuances of GenAI is indispensable for making substantive advancements in this burgeoning field. © 2026 Peter Lang Group AG, Lausanne (Switzerland) Published by Peter Lang GmbH, Berlin (Germany)
