AB  - [Objective] The strategic expansion of offshore wind power generation constitutes a critical pathway for achieving China's dual-carbon objectives, with flexible fractional-frequency transmission system (FFFTS) emerging as a technologically advanced solution for large-scale long-distance power transmission. These systems effectively integrate the advantages of both AC and DC transmission technologies, thereby addressing the key challenges in offshore energy evacuation. However, the reliable operation of FFFTSs fundamentally depends on the performance of protection schemes for their low-frequency transmission lines. A significant challenge arises from the system's inherent weak-infeed characteristics, stemming from the low inertia of wind power sources and the complexity of converter control strategies. These factors consequently cause substantial degradation in the sensitivity of conventional current differential protection schemes. This decline in sensitivity poses serious risks to sy
